---
import ConverterPage from '../components/ConverterPage.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is DevLys to Mangal the same as DevLys to Unicode?',
    a: 'Yes, and it is worth being clear about it. Mangal is a Unicode font that ships with Windows, not a separate encoding. "DevLys to Mangal" means "DevLys to Unicode, displayed in Mangal". The text this page produces is identical to what <a href="/devlys-to-unicode">DevLys to Unicode</a> produces - this page exists because government forms ask for Mangal by name.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do forms ask for Mangal specifically?',
    a: 'Because Mangal is installed on every Windows machine, so a department can be certain the Hindi will render on the clerk\'s computer, the reviewing officer\'s, and the portal that receives it. It is a safe default rather than a technical requirement. Any Unicode Devanagari font will display the same characters.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to install anything?',
    a: 'No. Mangal is already on Windows. On a Mac or phone the text will render in whatever Devanagari font the system has, and the characters are the same either way.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I set the font to Mangal in Word?',
    a: 'Paste the output, select it, and choose Mangal from the font list. Or press <strong>Download .docx</strong> on this page, which writes Mangal into the file so it opens correctly with no further steps.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will my English words, numbers and dates survive?',
    a: 'Yes. Dates, numbers, percentages, URLs and email addresses are always left untouched, and common English words are detected and preserved. The checkbox under the panels turns that behaviour off if you want everything converted.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my text get uploaded anywhere?',
    a: 'No. The conversion runs entirely in your browser and nothing is sent to a server, so office documents stay on your machine.',
  },
];
---

<ConverterPage
  path="/devlys-to-mangal"
  lede="Paste DevLys 010 text on the left and get Mangal-ready Unicode Hindi on the right, for government forms and online portals."
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>What "DevLys to Mangal" actually means</h2>
  <p>
    Mangal is not an encoding, it is a Unicode font that ships with Windows. So
    this page does the same work as
    <a href="/devlys-to-unicode">DevLys to Unicode</a> - it produces Unicode
    Hindi - and the only difference is the name on the label, because that is
    the name the form in front of you is using.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason the distinction matters: DevLys really <em>is</em> a different
    thing. It stores Hindi as ASCII and relies on the font to draw the letters.
    Mangal stores Hindi as Hindi. Going from one to the other is a genuine
    conversion with reordering involved. Going from Mangal to "Unicode" is not a
    conversion at all - it is the same text.
  </p>

  <h2>Why departments ask for Mangal</h2>
  <p>
    Because it is the safe choice. Mangal is present on every Windows install,
    so a department can be confident the Hindi will look the same on the
    typist's machine, the reviewing officer's, and the portal that receives the
    file. Nothing about the text requires Mangal specifically - any Unicode
    Devanagari font shows the same characters - but a form that says "Mangal"
    is asking for Unicode, and this page is how you give it to them.
  </p>

  <h2>What the conversion has to get right</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The short-i matra.</strong> DevLys types ि before its consonant
      because that is where it is drawn; Unicode puts it after. <code>fd</code>
      becomes <strong lang="hi">कि</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The reph.</strong> The र् above a letter is typed after its
      syllable, so <code>deZ</code> has to become
      <strong lang="hi">कर्म</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Split vowel signs.</strong> <code>dks</code> is an aa-matra plus an
      e-matra and has to be recomposed into <strong lang="hi">को</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Skip any of those and the text will still look like Hindi while failing
    search, sorting and spell-check inside the portal you are submitting to -
    which is the failure that costs you a resubmission.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Copy the Hindi out of your DevLys document.</li>
    <li>Paste it into the left panel; Unicode appears on the right.</li>
    <li>
      Press <strong>Copy text</strong>, paste into your form or Word file, and
      set the font to Mangal - or use <strong>Download .docx</strong>, which
      applies Mangal for you.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Going the other way, <a href="/mangal-to-devlys">Mangal to DevLys</a> turns
    Unicode back into DevLys keystrokes. If your source file is Kruti Dev rather
    than DevLys, use <a href="/kruti-dev-to-mangal">Kruti Dev to Mangal</a>.
  </p>
</ConverterPage>
